  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 355 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nWarm air advection will occur across the area on Saturday as\nhighs warm into the upper 80s to the middle 90s. Moisture will\nalso start to advect into the area from west to east Saturday \ninto Saturday night. Instability will increase across the area \nSaturday afternoon with the heating of a day. Lift will be \nlimited across the area but the cap should start to weaken \nSaturday afternoon. There will be the potential for a few \nisolated storms developing Saturday afternoon/early evening \nmainly west of Highway 65, as the cap will likely be stronger to\nthe east and the drier air mass may remain in place into the \nafternoon hours across the eastern Ozarks. Storm coverage will \nlikely be limited due to the weak lift therefore, most \nlocations will likely remain dry Saturday afternoon. If isolated\nstorms can develop there would be the potential for a few strong\nto severe storms with damaging winds the main risk due to the \nincreasing instability and Theta-E difference Saturday afternoon\nacross southeastern Kansas into western Missouri. If a storm can\ndevelop and produce an outflow, the low level flow would support\nadditional storm development on the outflow to the\neast/southeast. \n\nSaturday evening additional storm development will be possible\nacross central Kansas into northern Missouri. This activity \ncould develop into a complex of storms and track east southeast \nand move into portions of the area late Saturday evening into \nearly Sunday morning. The exact track will be dependent on \nexactly where storms develop and if the cold pools can congeal\nand storms developing into a complex. The storm motion would \nsupport a complex of storms moving east/southeast Saturday \nnight. Therefore, the further west storms develop the better \npotential more of the area is impacted, then further to the east\nand north would limit the potential. The better lift will \nremain to the north of the area but if a strong cold pool can \ndevelop low level wind flow and MUCAPE would support a complex \nof storms maintaining to the southeast into the area, again the \nexact track and locations impacted will be dependent on a \ncomplex of storms developing and exactly where one develops. \nThere will also be the potential for some strong to severe \nstorms with damaging winds the main risk with the Saturday night\nstorms too. \n\nHighs are expected to warm into the 90s again Sunday\ninto early next week. The ensemble model members are trending \nmore towards keeping the front further north through the middle\nof next week, which would keep highs in the 90s through the\nmiddle of next week. The front may then push into the area late\nnext week but there remains difference in the timing between the\nmodel members. There could be some scattered shower and storm \nchances at times early/middle of next week, but with the front \nremaining further north the widespread rain potential may remain\nmore limited to the front can move into and through the area.",
